-------------------------------------------------------------------

     =========================================================
     Geant4 - an Object-Oriented Toolkit for Simulation in HEP
     =========================================================
   
           
                           G4SKspallation
                           --------------

   This simulation demonstrates muon spallation processes in the SK Chrenkov water tank detector 
   with simple conditions for the geometry.

	
  1- GEOMETRY DEFINITION
	
   The geometry is constructed in the G4SKDetectorConstruction class.
   The setup consists of a an envelope (world) of box shape containing two volumes: 
   the water tank cylinder and the fiducial volume of the tank which is also a cylinder.
   ***Fiducial volume is commented - if you want to measure things only for the fiducial volume 
   you have to uncomment lines in the detector construction class.***

   
        |<---------- 42 m------------->|
        |                              |                       
        ================================ _ _
        ||water tank       	      ||   ^
        ||                   	      ||   |  
        ||  ========================  ||   |
        ||  |fiducial volume       |  ||   |
 muon   ||  |       	  	   |  ||   | 39 m
=======>||  |                      |  ||   |
        ||  |                	   |  ||   |
	||  ========================  ||   |
	||                     	      ||   v
	||                            || _ _
        ================================



   The envelope (world) is made of water (2.7 m water ~ 1 m rock) and the two inner volumes
   are also made of water.  

   The materials (water) are created with the help of the G4NistManager class,
   which allows to build a material from the NIST database using their
   names. All available materials can be found in the Geant4 User's Guide
   for Application Developers, Appendix 10: Geant4 Materials Database.
		
  2- PHYSICS LIST
 
   The particle's type and the physics processes which will be available in this simulation are
   set in the QGSP_BERT_HP physics list, or in the QGSP_BERT, or in the QGSP_BIC_HP, or in the 
   FTFP_BERT_HP, or in the QGSP_INCLXX_HP or in the FTFP_INCLXX_HP (for later comparison of 
   results of these different lists). These physics lists require data files for electromagnetic 
   and hadronic processes.
   See more on installation of the datasets in Geant4 Installation Guide,
   Chapter 3.3: Note On Geant4 Datasets:
   http://geant4.web.cern.ch/geant4/UserDocumentation/UsersGuides
                                           /InstallationGuide/html/ch03s03.html
   The following datasets: G4LEDATA, G4NDL4.5, G4INCL1.0, G4LEVELGAMMADATA, G4NEUTRONXSDATA,
   G4SAIDXSDATA and G4ENSDFSTATEDATA are mandatory for this simulation.

   In addition the build-in interactive command:
               /process/(in)activate processName
   allows to activate/inactivate the processes one by one.

   The physics list can be chosen by adding an extra argument when opening the application
   in batch mode. The second argument corresponds to the physics list choice. By default we 
   use the QGSP_BERT_HP. If the second argument is "0", we keep the QGSP_BERT_HP but without the 
   neutrons precision package, so actually we have the QGSP_BERT physics list. If it is "1", we 
   get the QGSP_BIC_HP. "2" brings the FTFP_BERT_HP, "3" the QGSP_INCLXX_HP and "4" the FTFP_INCLXX_HP. 
   Any other number or value for the second argument will bring the default physics list. 
   Meaning anything other than these numbers will be just a place holder. 
   
  3- ACTION INITALIZATION

   The newly introduced class, SKActionInitialization, 
   instantiates and registers to Geant4 kernel all user action classes.

   While in sequential mode the action classes are instatiated just once,
   via invoking the method:
      SKActionInitialization::Build() 
   in multi-threading mode the same method is invoked for each thread worker
   and so all user action classes are defined thread-local.

   A run action class is instantiated both thread-local 
   and global that's why its instance is created also in the method
      SKActionInitialization::BuildForMaster() 
   which is invoked only in multi-threading mode.
  	 
  4- PRIMARY GENERATOR
  
   The SKPrimaryGeneratorAction class introduces two different possibilities of primary particle generation.

   One possibility is the fixed energy and position generator.
   Its default kinematics is a ~271 GeV muon (which is the average muon energy for Super-Kamiokande) 
   with downward momentum along the cylinder axis located on top of the tank's water in the center
   of the tank surface. If the fiducial volume is activated, then its generated on top of the fiducial volume.

   This fixed primary generator is called by the 'batch' mode if we have no third argument. Or if we add a third
   argument consisting of "no" (case insensitive) the application still uses this generator.
   

   One may choose to read the initial conditions for the muons from an ASCII file. For this to be done,
   one have to keep the text file ini_cond.txt or add the initial condition file path in the third argument. 
   Then when lauching the application, if the command has a third argument the initial conditions will be 
   read from the given file with the help of the SKFileReader class. The muon will be generated
   with the energy and momentum direction given in the text file with a random position on the tank/fiducial 
   volume walls.
   The random position is generted by the projection of an uniformly random point inside the tank/fiducial volume
   on the walls of the volume in the direction of the initial muon momentum.
   
   For example, I want to keep the default physics list, read the run2.mac macro and use the initial conditions
   from the ini_cond.txt file. So I use the following command :
   % ./G4SKspallation run2.mac ! yes
   	***The last argument "yes"(case insensitive) make the application use the defult input 
	file (ini_cond.txt).***

   If the user wants to provide its own initial condtion file, just need to change the "yes" to "full_path_to_file".

   For example , I want to choose the QGSP_INCLXX_HP physics list, read the run3.mac macro and give my own initial
   conditions from the /home/user/SKsimu/my_ini_cond.txt file. So I use the following command :
   % ./G4SKspallation run3.mac 3 /home/user/SKsimu/my_ini_cond.txt

   Sometimes one may only want to set up the energy spectrum for the primary muons and not the full angular 
   distribution. In this case, one can add a fourth argument to the run command. This will make the particle 
   gun read only the energies from the input file and set the muon direction along the cylinder axis and its 
   initial position also at the axis on top of the tank/fiducial volume (same position generation of the other 
   generator). A place holder for this argument is "yes"(case insensitive).

   For example , I want to choose the QGSP_INCLXX_HP physics list, read the run1.mac macro, give my own initial
   conditions from the /home/user/SKsimu/my_ini_cond.txt file and deactivate the angular distribution. So I use 
   the following command :
   % ./G4SKspallation run3.mac 3 /home/user/SKsimu/my_ini_cond.txt no
   	***The last argument "no" could be anything excep "yes"(case insensitive).***

   The initial condition file ini_cond.txt is the default initial condition file for the interactive mode.
   Also for the interactive mode, the initial particles are muon minus with angular distribution activated 
   and it uses the default physics list.

   The FileReader class, in multi-threading mode, reads the number of threads times the events required per run, 
   because each thread gets an initial condition when it starts a new event and the speed that each thread ends
   the simulation of an event is not always equal to the other threads speeds, mainly because they have different 
   initial conditions, so one thread can read much more initial conditions than others. So as the FileReader is 
   thread local, each working thread must have enough conditions for simulating the entire run.

   By default, the muon generated is a muon minus. To generate muons plus, add a fifth argument when running the
   simulation in batch mode (the interactive mode has its commands to change the initial particle in the gun). 
   Any fifth argument will activate muons plus. A place holder for the muons plus is "no"(case insensitive) or
   "-" (the minus sign).
  
   For example , I want to keep the default physics list, read the run1.mac macro and generate constant
   energies and initial positions muons plus. So I use the following command :
   % ./G4SKspallation run1.mac ! no anything +
   	***The last argument "+" could be anything.***
   Or maybe read the initial conditions file for the muons plus :
   % ./G4SKspallation run1.mac ! yes yes +
   	***The last argument "+" could be anything. And the fourth argument "yes"(case insensitive) is just a 
           place holder.***


   This default settings can be changed via the Geant4 built-in commands of the G4ParticleGun class.
     
  5- DETECTOR RESPONSE

   This simulation contains two data retrieving methods. 

   One is the command-based method implemented in the main function (G4SKspallation.cc) with the line : 
   G4ScoringManager * scManager = G4ScoringManager::GetScoringManager();  
   And then with scoring macros. You would have to build your own macro, but this is quite simple.
   The scoring macro available already have most of the examples needed for you to construct your
   own personal macro. - This method is recommended for fast checks as it is the most straight forward
   way of collecting data from the simulated events.
	***This method is desactivated (commented for now) in the main function - to activate only
	   uncomment two lines in the G4SKspallation.cc and write the macros***

   The other method is introduced with the definition of user's actions (user's hooks). This is more complex
   and , for this specific simulation , it requires the definition of the following classes :
   SKAnalysis, SKRunAction, SKEventAction and SKSteppingAction.
   And also some changes in the action initialization and the detector geometry classes. Along with introduction
   of some commands in the main function.
   
   If there is a sixth argument when calling the simulation in 'batch' mode, the data is only saved for events in 
   which an isotope with atomic mass greater or equal 8 was produced (this condition can be changed in the 
   SKSteppingAction class). 
   
   For example , I want to keep the default physics list, to read the run1.mac macro, to generate constant
   energies and initial positions muons minus and to save data only for events in which an isotope with 
   atomic mass greater or equal 8 was produced. So I use the following command :
   % ./G4SKspallation run1.mac ! no anything - yes 
   	***The last argument "yes" could be anything.***
   
   The energy deposited in the detector volume is collected step by step in a SKSteppingAction function and accumulated 
   for an event in a SKEventAction class variable. The tank is the sensitive/tracking volume for retrieving data (one
   can change the volume to be only the fiducial volume by defining the fiducial volume - uncomment - in the detector 
   geometry class). The muon initial kinetic energy is also kept in a SKEventAction class variable as well as its total
   path length inside the detector. Some vectors for secondary particles information are also kept in the 
   SKEventAction class object that is passed to the SKSteppingAction class at its construction.

   At end of an event, the values acummulated in the SKEventAction class objects are added to histograms and to the ntuple
   row corresponding to this event in a SKEventAction class function. These histograms and the ntuple are created in the 
   SKRunAction.

   Some data may also be added to histograms already within SKSteppingAction class functions. This is done for the 2D 
   histogram of atomic mass A and atomic number Z of ions produced. 

   These histograms and the ntuple are all saved in a root file named "SK.root" (it can be changed to a csv or a xml file
   in the SKAnalysis class) in a function of the SKRunAction class called at the end of a run. This file is created at 
   the beginning of a run within this same class (SKRunAction).

   To change the information collected, you can change or add new ntuple collumns and histograms in the SKRunAction 
   class. Then you have to fill these data structures within the SKSteppingAction or SKEventAction classes. Usually, you 
   will collect the information from the Step or Track object in the SKSteppingAction::UserSteppingAction function. Then 
   you will either save it directly in the data structures or accumulate it per event in SKEventAction class objects and 
   save it in the data structures within the SKEventAction class function called at the end of each event. 

   If you read a bit these three classes mentioned in the previous paragraph you will understand the logics 
   behind this data retrieving method and you will be able to collect the information you want. 

   More information about the kind of information you can collect from Track or Step objets can be
   found in the following source code websites :

   Track object : https://geant4.kek.jp/lxr/source/track/include/G4Track.hh 
   Step object  : https://geant4.kek.jp/lxr/source/track/include/G4Step.hh 

   Alternative ways of scoring via Geant4 classes can be found in many Geant4 official examples.

  A- VISUALISATION

   The visualization manager is set via the G4VisExecutive class
   in the main() function in G4SKspallation.cc.    
   The initialisation of the drawing is done via a set of /vis/ commands
   in the macro vis.mac. This macro is automatically read from
   the main function when the simulation is used in interactive running mode.

   By default, vis.mac opens an OpenGL viewer (/vis/open OGL).
   The user can change the initial viewer by commenting out this line
   and instead uncommenting one of the other /vis/open statements, such as
   HepRepFile or DAWNFILE (which produce files that can be viewed with the
   HepRApp and DAWN viewers, respectively).  Note that one can always
   open new viewers at any time from the command line.  For example, if
   you already have a view in, say, an OpenGL window with a name
   "viewer-0", then
      /vis/open DAWNFILE
   then to get the same view
      /vis/viewer/copyView viewer-0
   or to get the same view *plus* scene-modifications
      /vis/viewer/set/all viewer-0
   then to see the result
      /vis/viewer/flush

   The DAWNFILE, HepRepFile drivers are always available
   (since they require no external libraries), but the OGL driver requires
   that the Geant4 libraries have been built with the OpenGL option.

   The vis.mac macro in G4SKspallation has additional commands
   that demonstrate additional functionality of the vis system, such as
   displaying text, axes, scales, date, logo and shows how to change
   viewpoint and style.  To see even more commands use help or
   ls or browse the available UI commands in the Application
   Developers Guide, Section 7.1.

   For more information on visualization, including information on how to
   install and run DAWN, OpenGL and HepRApp, see the visualization tutorials,
   for example,
   http://geant4.slac.stanford.edu/Presentations/vis/G4[VIS]Tutorial/G4[VIS]Tutorial.html
   (where [VIS] can be replaced by DAWN, OpenGL and HepRApp)

   The tracks are automatically drawn at the end of each event, accumulated
   for all events and erased at the beginning of the next run.

 B- USER INTERFACES
 
   The user command interface is set via the G4UIExecutive class
   in the main() function in G4SKspallation.cc .
   The selection of the user command interface is then done automatically 
   according to the Geant4 configuration or it can be done explicitly via 
   the third argument of the G4UIExecutive constructor (see exampleB4a.cc 
   of the Geant4-10.06.p01 examples distributed with the Geant4 source). 
 
 ###C- HOW TO RUN

    - Execute G4SKspallation in the 'interactive mode' with visualization:
        % ./G4SKspallation
      and type in the commands line by line:  
        Idle> /control/verbose 2
        Idle> /tracking/verbose 1
        Idle> /run/beamOn 1
        Idle> ...
        Idle> exit
      or
        Idle> /control/execute run1.mac
        ....
        Idle> exit

    - Execute G4SKspallation in the 'batch' mode from macro files 
      (without visualization)
        % ./G4SKspallation run2.mac

    - Change the physics list in the 'batch' mode with a second argument : "0" for QGSP_BERT,
    "1" for QGSP_BIC_HP , "2" for FTFP_BERT_HP, "3" for QGSP_INCLXX_HP and "4" for 
    FTFP_INCLXX_HP.

    - Choose to read your initial conditions from txt file by adding any third argument if 
    different than "no" (case insensitive). 
    If this argument is "yes" we read the conditions from the default file (ini_cond.txt) which
    has the conditions of background muons arriving at SK. 
    Another possibility is adding the path to you own file in this argument. So you will read the 
    conditions from that file.
	 ***It only reads the energy spectrum if there is a fourth argument - except if this 
	 	argument is "yes" (case insensitive).***

    - Choose to use muons plus and not muons minus for initial particles with any fifth argument, 
    except if the argument is "no" (case insensitive) or "-" (the minus sign).
    
    - Choose to only save data from events in which isotopes with atomic masses greater or equal 
    to 8 are produced with the addition of a sixth argument. 

    Example of a run in batch mode using a given initial conditions file, the FTFP_BERT_HP physics list
    , reading the angular distribution for muons plus, saving data only for events with the production of 
    "heavy" isotopes and running the run2.mac :

    % ./G4SKspallation run2.mac 2 /home/user/file.txt yes + yes

    *** The first argument "run2.mac" tells which macro to run. The second argument tells that we are using 
    		the /home/user/file.txt as initial conditions file. The "yes" (case insensitive) for the 
		fourth argument is a place holder to keep the reading of the angles. The before last 
		argument "+" could be anything except to "no" (case insensitive) and "-" (minus sign).
		The last argument makes us save only events with "heavy" isotopes produced and it could 
		be anything.***

  D - RUN MACROS

    - run1.mac : This is made for the interactive mode because it is a very small run, 4 events. These 4 muons
    have energies and angles given by the ini_cond.txt and their trajectories will be drawn if you are in 
    visualization and interactive mode.

    - run2.mac : This is made for the batch mode because it is a bit bigger, 2 runs of 10 events. These 20 
    muons will have the conditions you want by choosing the arguments you give when calling the simulation 
    in batch mode. This macro is generally used for testing the simulation in a fast way after building it.

    - run3.mac : This is made for the batch mode with no doubt, because this one is big, 10000 events.
    These 10k muons will have the conditions you want by choosing the arguments you give when calling the 
    batch mode. This is generally used for having the first figures tests.
    
  E - MuLTI-THREADING
 
     - Please note that the pre-processor flag G4MULTITHREADED is set when the Geant4 library is built with the 
     CMAKE option GEANT4_BUILD_MULTITHREADED=ON. So to activate the multi-threading mode you have to build your 
     Geant4 with this cmake option ON : 
     
     $ -DGEANT4_BUILD_MULTITHREADED=ON .
     
     - With the multi-threading option ON, you can still build the simulation in sequential mode with small changes
     in the G4SKspallation.cc code (and in the class where the G4MULTITHREADED flag appears). For this, you may use 
     the G4RunManager as run manager and not the G4MTRunManager, or only set the number of threads to 1 in the main 
     function G4SKspallation.cc .
     
     - If you do not activate the cmake option when building the Geant4, the simulation will run normally in sequential
     mode with no need of further changes.
     
     
End.
